§§ `4 Procedure of tribunal`

-- 4(1): a sitting is presided over by (a) the chair, (b) the vice-chair if the chair is absent, or (c) if both are absent, a member appointed for the purpose by the members present at the sitting.
-- Where an office-holder stands at the sitting, is absent from it, or the office is vacant. F9.15: a vacant office of chair or vice-chair counts as absent (reading A, taken), or the paragraph does not cover it and the Law gives no answer (reading B).
DECLARE `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting` IS ONE OF
    `at the sitting`
    `absent from the sitting`
    `the office is vacant`

DECLARE `Person presiding over a sitting of the tribunal` IS ONE OF
    `the chair`
    `the vice-chair`
    `a member appointed for the purpose by the members present at the sitting`

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(1) (src:846)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      status IS A `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 2 para 4(1) the office-holder is absent for the purposes of paragraph 4(1)` interp status MEANS
    CONSIDER status
    WHEN `at the sitting` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `absent from the sitting` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the office is vacant`
        THEN CONSIDER interp's `a vacant office of chair or vice-chair`
             WHEN `a vacant office counts as absent` THEN `F9.15 reading A: a vacant office counts as absent`
             WHEN `a vacant office is not covered and the Law does not answer` THEN `F9.15 reading B: a vacant office is not covered and the Law does not answer`

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(1) (src:846)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.15 reading A: a vacant office counts as absent` MEANS
    TRUE

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(1) (src:846)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.15 reading B: a vacant office is not covered and the Law does not answer` MEANS
    REFUSE "paragraph 4(1) speaks of the chair and the vice-chair being absent and does not say who presides where the office of chair or vice-chair is vacant"

-- src:846 | (1) A sitting of the tribunal is to be presided over by –
-- src:847 | (a) the chair;
-- src:848 | (b) the vice-chair, if the chair is absent; or
-- src:849 | (c) if both the chair and vice-chair are absent, a tribunal member appointed for the purpose by the tribunal members present at the sitting.
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(1) (src:846-849)
@export Who presides over a sitting of the tribunal: the chair; the vice-chair if the chair is absent; or, if both are absent, a member appointed for the purpose by the members present
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2` @desc The readings chosen for the counting of every period in Schedules 1 and 2 (the favoured reading of each fork is the default)
      chair IS A `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting` @desc Whether the chair is at the sitting, absent from it, or the office of chair is vacant
      `vice-chair` IS A `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting` @desc Whether the vice-chair is at the sitting, absent from it, or the office of vice-chair is vacant
GIVETH A `Person presiding over a sitting of the tribunal`
`Sch 2 para 4(1) the person presiding over a sitting` interp chair `vice-chair` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `Sch 2 para 4(1) the office-holder is absent for the purposes of paragraph 4(1)` interp chair
           THEN `the chair`
           IF NOT `Sch 2 para 4(1) the office-holder is absent for the purposes of paragraph 4(1)` interp `vice-chair`
           THEN `the vice-chair`
           OTHERWISE `a member appointed for the purpose by the members present at the sitting`

-- src:847 | (a) the chair;
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(1)(a) (src:847)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      chair IS A `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 2 para 4(1)(a) the chair presides` interp chair MEANS
    NOT `Sch 2 para 4(1) the office-holder is absent for the purposes of paragraph 4(1)` interp chair

-- src:848 | (b) the vice-chair, if the chair is absent; or
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(1)(b) (src:848)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      chair IS A `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting`
      `vice-chair` IS A `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 2 para 4(1)(b) the vice-chair presides, the chair being absent` interp chair `vice-chair` MEANS
        `Sch 2 para 4(1) the office-holder is absent for the purposes of paragraph 4(1)` interp chair
    AND NOT `Sch 2 para 4(1) the office-holder is absent for the purposes of paragraph 4(1)` interp `vice-chair`

-- src:849 | (c) if both the chair and vice-chair are absent, a tribunal member appointed for the purpose by the tribunal members present at the sitting.
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(1)(c) (src:849)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2`
      chair IS A `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting`
      `vice-chair` IS A `Whether a chair or vice-chair is at a sitting`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 2 para 4(1)(c) a member appointed by the members present presides, both the chair and the vice-chair being absent` interp chair `vice-chair` MEANS
        `Sch 2 para 4(1) the office-holder is absent for the purposes of paragraph 4(1)` interp chair
    AND `Sch 2 para 4(1) the office-holder is absent for the purposes of paragraph 4(1)` interp `vice-chair`

-- 4(2): subject to 4(1) and to any Regulations under 4(3), the tribunal may regulate its own procedure: it may do so for a matter that neither 4(1) nor Regulations deal with.
-- src:850 | (2) Subject to sub-paragraph (1), and to any Regulations under sub-paragraph (3), the tribunal may regulate its own procedure.
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(2) (src:850)
@export Whether the tribunal may regulate a matter of its procedure itself: it may, unless paragraph 4(1) or Regulations under paragraph 4(3) deal with the matter
GIVEN `paragraph 4(1) deals with the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc TRUE if sub-paragraph (1) (who presides at a sitting) deals with the matter
      `Regulations under paragraph 4(3) deal with the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc TRUE if Regulations under sub-paragraph (3) deal with the matter (an input: the Regulations are not among the sources)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 2 para 4(2) the tribunal may regulate the matter by its own procedure` `paragraph 4(1) deals with the matter` `Regulations under paragraph 4(3) deal with the matter` MEANS
        NOT `paragraph 4(1) deals with the matter`
    AND NOT `Regulations under paragraph 4(3) deal with the matter`

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(2) (src:850)
@export The tribunal's power to regulate its own procedure on a matter that neither paragraph 4(1) nor Regulations deal with
GIVEN `paragraph 4(1) deals with the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc TRUE if sub-paragraph (1) (who presides at a sitting) deals with the matter
      `Regulations under paragraph 4(3) deal with the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc TRUE if Regulations under sub-paragraph (3) deal with the matter (an input: the Regulations are not among the sources)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Schedule 2`
`Sch 2 para 4(2) subject to sub-paragraph (1) and to any Regulations under sub-paragraph (3) the tribunal may regulate its own procedure` `paragraph 4(1) deals with the matter` `Regulations under paragraph 4(3) deal with the matter` MEANS
    PARTY `the Charity Tribunal`
    MAY `Sch 2: the tribunal regulates its own procedure`
    PROVIDED `Sch 2 para 4(2) the tribunal may regulate the matter by its own procedure` `paragraph 4(1) deals with the matter` `Regulations under paragraph 4(3) deal with the matter`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST FULFILLED

-- 4(3): the States may by Regulations make further provision as to the procedure of the tribunal, including on ten matters. The Regulations are not among the sources: their content is an input.
-- src:851 | (3) The States may by Regulations make further provision as to the procedure of the tribunal, including in particular provision as to –
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3) (src:851)
@export The States' power to make Regulations on the procedure of the tribunal
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Schedule 2`
`Sch 2 para 4(3) the States may by Regulations make further provision as to the procedure of the tribunal` MEANS
    PARTY `the States`
    MAY `Sch 2: the States make Regulations on the procedure of the tribunal`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST FULFILLED

-- (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), (g), (h), (i), (j) are matters the Regulations may cover: inert, since the Regulations are not in the sources and the Law attaches no rule of its own to the matters.
