@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(a) (src:852)
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(a) the burden of proof on an appeal (inert)` MEANS
    "(a) the burden of proof on an appeal;"
-- src:853 | (b) the summoning of witnesses and the administration of oaths;
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(b) (src:853)
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(b) the summoning of witnesses and the administration of oaths (inert)` MEANS
    "(b) the summoning of witnesses and the administration of oaths;"
-- src:854 | (c) securing the production of documents and evidence;
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(c) (src:854)
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(c) securing the production of documents and evidence (inert)` MEANS
    "(c) securing the production of documents and evidence;"
-- src:855 | (d) the conduct of hearings, the determination of appeals with or without hearings, and the holding of hearings wholly or partly in public or private or in the absence of any party;
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(d) (src:855)
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(d) the conduct of hearings and the determination of appeals (inert)` MEANS
    "(d) the conduct of hearings, the determination of appeals with or without hearings, and the holding of hearings wholly or partly in public or private or in the absence of any party;"
-- (e) names "quorum or voting rules" as a matter for Regulations; the Law itself states none, which is encoded as a named refusal just below.
-- src:856 | (e) enabling an appeal to be determined, or any matter preliminary or incidental to an appeal to be dealt with or direction given, by the chair or vice-chair, or subject to any quorum or voting rules;
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(e) (src:856)
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(e) determination of an appeal by the chair or vice-chair, and quorum and voting rules (inert)` MEANS
    "(e) enabling an appeal to be determined, or any matter preliminary or incidental to an appeal to be dealt with or direction given, by the chair or vice-chair, or subject to any quorum or voting rules;"

-- The quorum of a sitting, and the voting rule, are for Regulations under 4(3)(e); the Law fixes neither. A question that asks for the quorum is answered with a refusal, not with a number.
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(e) (src:856)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Sch 2 para 4 the quorum of the tribunal is not stated in the Law` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Law states no quorum for a sitting of the tribunal: any quorum or voting rule is for Regulations under Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(e), which are not among the sources"

-- (f): the Regulations may provide for costs, and may create offences in relation to the tribunal, carrying a penalty not exceeding imprisonment for a term of 2 years and a fine.
-- The cap on the content of the Regulations: imprisonment up to 24 months (reading A of F9.13, calendar months) or up to 730 days (reading B, counted from a day), and any fine ("a fine" states no amount).
-- src:857 | (f) the award of costs, and the creation of offences in relation to the tribunal, carrying a penalty not exceeding imprisonment for a term of 2 years and a fine;
@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(f) (src:857)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(f) the penalty may not exceed imprisonment for this many months` MEANS 24

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(f) (src:857)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(f) the penalty may not exceed imprisonment for this many days` MEANS 730

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(f) (src:857)
@export Whether the penalty that Regulations under paragraph 4(3)(f) attach to an offence in relation to the tribunal is within the cap of imprisonment for a term of 2 years and a fine (24 months, or 730 days on the days reading)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2` @desc The readings chosen for the counting of every period in Schedules 1 and 2 (the favoured reading of each fork is the default)
      penalty IS A `Penalty provided` @desc The penalty the Regulations provide: a term of imprisonment in months, if any, and a fine
      `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run` IS A DATE @desc Used only on the reading that counts the cap in days: the day from which the term of imprisonment would run
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(f) the penalty is within the cap` interp penalty `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run` MEANS
    CONSIDER penalty's `imprisonment up to, in months`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
    WHEN JUST months
        THEN CONSIDER interp's `the unit of the cap on imprisonment`
             WHEN `calendar months` THEN `F9.13 reading A: the term is within the cap when its months are at most 24` months
             WHEN `days` THEN `F9.13 reading B: the term is within the cap when its days are at most 730` months `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run`

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(f) (src:857)
GIVEN months IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.13 reading A: the term is within the cap when its months are at most 24` months MEANS
    months AT MOST `Sch 2 para 4(3)(f) the penalty may not exceed imprisonment for this many months`

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(f) (src:857)
GIVEN months IS A NUMBER
      `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`F9.13 reading B: the term is within the cap when its days are at most 730` months `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run` MEANS
    (Day (`add months` `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run` months) MINUS Day `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run`) AT MOST `Sch 2 para 4(3)(f) the penalty may not exceed imprisonment for this many days`

@ref Schedule 2 paragraph 4(3)(f) (src:857)
@export The States' power to create, by Regulations, offences in relation to the tribunal with a penalty not exceeding imprisonment for 2 years and a fine
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Schedules 1 and 2` @desc The readings chosen for the counting of every period in Schedules 1 and 2 (the favoured reading of each fork is the default)
      penalty IS A `Penalty provided` @desc The penalty the Regulations provide: a term of imprisonment in months, if any, and the fine
      `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run` IS A DATE @desc Used only on the reading that counts the cap in days: the day from which the term would run
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Schedule 2`
`Sch 2 para 4(3)(f) the States may by Regulations provide for costs and create offences in relation to the tribunal with a penalty not exceeding imprisonment for 2 years and a fine` interp penalty `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run` MEANS
    PARTY `the States`
    MAY `Sch 2: the States make Regulations creating an offence in relation to the tribunal`
    PROVIDED `Sch 2 para 4(3)(f) the penalty is within the cap` interp penalty `the day from which the term of imprisonment would run`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST FULFILLED

